          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "I’m Up and Down, and Round and Round"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.855,
            0.068,
            0.884,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "95"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.887,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Are there more circles passing through both A and B? Clearly, any point equidistant from A and B can be the centre of a circle passing through A and B. Do you know of such points? We have seen that the perpendicular bisector of the line segment AB is the locus of points equidistant from A and B. Every point on the perpendicular bisector is equidistant from A and B, and every point that is equidistant from A and B is on the perpendicular bisector! So, the centres of all circles through A and B lie on the perpendicular bisector of AB."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.27,
            0.579,
            0.557
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.123,
                0.27,
                0.579,
                0.557
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_3_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.198,
                0.565,
                0.506,
                0.583
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.4: Circles through two points"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.587,
            0.31,
            0.88,
            0.544
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In Fig. 5.4, we see circles through points A and B, and through C and D, and the perpendicular bisector of CD. Every point on the perpendicular bisector is the centre of a circle passing through C and D. So we have circles with centres K, J and L containing points C and D."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.605,
            0.399,
            0.629
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.643,
            0.703,
            0.663
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. How many circles pass through two points on a plane?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.666,
            0.855,
            0.725
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Are there circles of all possible radii passing through A and B? What is the radius of the smallest circle passing through A and B? What is the radius of the largest circle passing through A and B?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.728,
            0.855,
            0.767
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. As you move away from segment AB along its perpendicular bisector, do the radii of the circles containing A and B increase or decrease?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.771,
            0.855,
            0.81
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. As you go along the perpendicular bisector, will the circle drawn from that point through A and B appear more curved or less curved?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.813,
            0.856,
            0.89
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. You are given two points A and B on a plane. How many squares can you draw on the same plane with A and B on the boundary? How many squares can you draw on the plane with A and B as the corners of the square?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
